Also not quite right — `captureUnexpectedError` already does this filtering internally, it's not a bare `Sentry.captureException` wrapper.

Look at `src/lib/observability/sentry.ts:228-234`:
```ts
export const captureUnexpectedError = (error, context = {}): boolean => {
    if (isExpectedError(error)) {
        return false;
    }
    ...
```

And `isExpectedError` (`src/lib/errors/AppError.ts:361-371`) defers to each `AppError`'s `expected` flag. `ValidationError`, `NotFoundError`, `ConflictError`, and `AuthError` all default to `expected: true` in their constructors (`AppError.ts:154`, `178`, `227`, `204`) — which is exactly what `getValidInvitation` throws for expired/used/missing-player-reference/email-conflict cases. So those never reach `Sentry.captureException`; the call is a no-op for them. Only genuinely unexpected failures — `InternalError`, `ExternalServiceError` (both `expected: false`), or a raw non-`AppError` throw like a DB timeout — actually get sent.

This mirrors the existing pattern in the shared verify route (`route.ts:39-49`, tested explicitly in `route.vitest.spec.ts:93-110` — "does not capture expected domain errors") and in `admin/users/page.tsx:19`. Calling it unconditionally on every caught error is the established idiom here, not a bug — the function's job is to be the filter, so callers don't have to duplicate `isExpectedError` checks themselves. No change needed.
